    "result_text": "The full test suite passes. Here's a summary of the change I implemented.\n\n## What was required\nReturn `namesakeCount` on an owner = the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively.\n\n## Changes\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` field mapped to the `namesake_count` column, with getter/setter.\n\n2. **Database schemas** \u2014 added a `namesake_count` column to all four `schema.sql` files (h2, hsqldb, postgres, mysql). Tests run on hsqldb.\n\n3. **OpenAPI spec** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the field and MapStruct maps it automatically (matching field name).\n\n4. **New pipeline step** (`CountOwnerNamesakes.java`) \u2014 reads the built `Owner` via `@Val`, counts existing owners in the repository whose lower-cased firstName+lastName match (skipping the record being created), and stamps the count on the owner in place. Because it runs before `SaveOwner`, the count reflects only pre-existing owners.\n\n5. **Endpoint wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted a `countNamesakes` step between `checkHousehold` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance as the surrounding steps.\n\nThe count is computed at create time, persisted, and returned via `GET /api/owners/{id}`, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (one small function class, `@Val`/`Out` state hand-off, no `@RestController`). `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` and the full suite are green.",
